Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Problem: plant-incident.html, cyber-incident.html, and supplier-shutdown.html
all wrote to the exact same key, TSM_HONEYWELL_RELAY. Whichever scenario a
user ran last silently clobbered the other two.

Fix:
1. relay.core.js: replaced the single HONEYWELL registry entry with three
   distinct domains — HONEYWELL_CYBER, HONEYWELL_SUPPLIER, HONEYWELL_PLANT.

2. plant-incident.html / cyber-incident.html / supplier-shutdown.html: each
   now writes to its own key instead of the shared TSM_HONEYWELL_RELAY,
   and also mirrors the write through window.TSM.relay.write().

3. honeywell-strategist.html: the source branch split the write side but
   left loadRelay() reading only the old single key, which nothing writes
   to post-split — would have silently broken the strategist entirely.
   Rewrote loadRelay() to check all three domain keys and return whichever
   has the most recent timestamp, with the old key as a one-time fallback
   read. Updated the storage-event listener accordingly and added the
   missing relay.core.js script include.

Verified: server boots clean; all 5 files serve 200; relay.core.js
registry contains all three new domain keys; each scenario file confirmed
writing to its own distinct key; strategist confirmed reading from all
three domain keys.
